  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "One piece of the puzzle when trying to end ripping is the vrc id. Currently it can be stored, shared, and queried at any time.\nMy suggestion is the following:\n1. Stop using VRC ID for downloading other user's avatars.\n2. Make VRC ID a unique single-use token.\n3. Require a request from the downloading client to the vrc server to get a unique token. the request then informs the user and via safety settings the user can automatically allow or decline the request. Allowing will create a single use token which represents the avatar's vrc id in vrchat server. The id will also only last for 5 minutes. Declining will instead present the \"fallback avatar\" which is always shared.\n\nThe safety setting feature will allow the user to define who gets to see their private avatars giving users more control and agency over protecting their own personal avatars. Some may not care and some may feel so concerned that they can block everyone and only allow specific users with a manual \"Allow Avatar Download\".\n\nThis is only one piece of the puzzle and I wish to share this to spark ideas you may have on how to implement new features to protect our (creators) IP.\n\nThank you.",
